<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kulob</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kulyab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kulob is a city in Khatlon province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, centre of a historic region east of the Vakhsh river. Once a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, the region was in the civil war of 1992–1997 the base of the Popular Front that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emomali-rahmon">Emomali Rahmon</a>, a native of the area, to power. People from Kulob have since dominated the country's leadership.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kulob.jpg" alt="Kulob" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maris Teteris · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulob_2700_th_Anniversary_monument_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kulob is a city in Khatlon Region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, 203 km south-east of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> on the river Yakhsu, a right tributary of the Panj. It is one of the largest cities in the country, with a population estimated at 106,300 for the city proper and 214,700 with its outlying communities (2020). It has a hot-summer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, with an annual average temperature of 15.8 °C.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> the region was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>. A Greek inscription of 200–195 BCE records a man named Heliodotos dedicating a fire altar to Hestia for the sake of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i">Euthydemus I</a> and his son Demetrius I. The historian al-Tabari mentions Khatlon as early as 737 CE, though the city's founding is said to be much earlier. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-sayyid-ali-hamadani">Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani</a>, who died while travelling through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in 1384, was buried in Khatlon, and his tomb still stands.</p><p>The city was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and then formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>. It was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> in 1559 and after the eighteenth century usually sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Its name was changed to Kulob in 1750. In 1861 the local lord Surrah Khan imprisoned Abdul Mejid, a British and Kokand agent, on behalf of Bukhara, before disorder in the emirate led him to release the party.</p><p>Agreements between the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empires</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> brought Kulob and its hinterland under the Russian Empire. After the Russian Revolution it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> only in March 1921 and in 1929 became part of the Tajik Soviet Socialist Republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>During the Tajikistani Civil War in the early 1990s the city was the main base of the Popular Front militias. Danghara, a village in the Kulob area, is the birthplace of the president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emomali-rahmon">Emomali Rahmon</a>. After independence in 1991 the Russian 201st Motor Rifle Division was stationed in Kulob, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> and Qurghonteppa. In September 2006 the city marked its 2,700th anniversary. It is served by Kulob Airport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kulob” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An inscription from the <b>Kulob</b> area, dated 200–195 BCE, calls Euthydemus the greatest of all kings and his son Demetrius I Demetrios Kallinikos, the Glorious Conqueror.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Tension between the old communist elite, largely from Khujand and <b>Kulob</b>, and an opposition of democrats, Islamists and Pamiris from Gharm and Gorno-Badakhshan erupted into civil war in 1992.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></li></ul></section>
